  "Question": "If \\(t=\\dfrac1{1-\\sqrt[4]{2}},\\) then \\(t\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((1-\\sqrt[4]{2})(2-\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "B": "\\((1-\\sqrt[4]{2})(1+\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "C": "\\((1+\\sqrt[4]{2})(1-\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "D": "\\((1+\\sqrt[4]{2})(1+\\sqrt2)\\)",
    "E": "\\(-(1+\\sqrt[4]{2})(1+\\sqrt2)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Let \\(r=\\sqrt[4]{2}=\\sqrt{\\sqrt2}\\), so \\(r^2=\\sqrt2\\) and \\(r^4=2\\). Multiplying by \\(1+r\\) replaces \\(r\\) in the denominator by \\(r^2\\). A second difference of squares then removes the remaining radical.\n\nSince \\(r>1\\), the denominators below are nonzero. We obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nt&=\\frac1{1-r}\\\\\n&=\\frac{1+r}{1-r^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(1+r)(1+r^2)}{1-r^4}\\\\\n&=-(1+r)(1+\\sqrt2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus \\(t=-(1+\\sqrt[4]{2})(1+\\sqrt2)\\), choice E.",
